P.append(("P01","Biology","""Most animals that live through northern winters avoid freezing in one of two ways: they migrate, or they shelter somewhere the temperature stays above the freezing point of their body fluids. The wood frog of North America, whose range reaches north of the Arctic Circle in Alaska and Canada, does neither. It spends the winter beneath a shallow layer of leaf litter, and when the temperature falls far enough, it freezes.

The freezing is not uniform. Ice forms in the spaces outside the cells, in the body cavity, and beneath the skin, while the fluid inside the cells stays liquid. In this state, which can last for weeks, the frog's heart stops beating and it does not breathe. Survival depends on what happens when ice first begins to form on the skin. Within minutes, the liver starts converting its stored glycogen into large amounts of glucose, which the blood carries to the organs before circulation stops. The sugar acts as a cryoprotectant: by raising the concentration of dissolved substances inside the cells, it limits how much water is drawn out of them as the surrounding fluid turns to ice, so that the cells do not shrink to a fatal degree.

There are limits to this tolerance. Laboratory studies suggest that frogs generally survive when up to roughly two-thirds of their body water has frozen, but not much more. In spring the ice melts, the heart begins beating again, and the frog soon moves on to breed in temporary ponds.""",[
 Q("The word \"tolerance\" in the passage is closest in meaning to","hard",["willingness to accept opinions different from one's own","permitted variation in a measured value","ability to survive a harmful condition","reduced response to a drug after repeated use"],2,"\"This tolerance\" refers back to the frog's capacity to live through being frozen, and the next sentence gives its limit (about two-thirds of body water frozen).",["The passage is about surviving freezing, not about accepting other people's views.","No measurement or engineering allowance is discussed.","No drug or repeated dosing is involved; the frog is enduring ice."]),
 Q("According to paragraph 2, when does the liver begin producing large amounts of glucose?","medium",["shortly after ice starts to form on the skin","after the heart has stopped beating","when the frog first buries itself beneath the leaf litter","as the ice in the body begins to melt"],0,"\"Survival depends on what happens when ice first begins to form on the skin. Within minutes, the liver starts converting its stored glycogen into ... glucose.\"",["The glucose is carried to the organs \"before circulation stops\", so production begins before the heart stops.","Burial in leaf litter is described in paragraph 1 and is not linked to glucose.","Melting happens in spring (paragraph 3), long after the glucose is produced."]),
 Q("Why does the author mention that the wood frog's range \"reaches north of the Arctic Circle\"?","medium",["To suggest that the frog's range has recently expanded northward","To explain why the frog migrates south before winter","To show that the frog breeds in temporary ponds","To stress that the frog endures long, severe winters"],3,"The detail sets up the contrast in paragraph 1: animals usually migrate or shelter from freezing, but this frog, living in very cold regions, \"does neither\".",["No change in the frog's range over time is mentioned.","The passage says the frog does not migrate: it \"does neither\".","Breeding in ponds is mentioned only in paragraph 3 and is unrelated to the frog's range."]),
 Q("According to the passage, all of the following happen to a wood frog while it is frozen EXCEPT:","hard",["Ice forms in the body cavity and beneath the skin.","The frog's heart stops beating for a time.","The fluid inside its cells turns to ice.","The frog stops breathing."],2,"The passage says \"the fluid inside the cells stays liquid\"; only the fluid outside the cells freezes.",["Stated: ice forms \"in the body cavity, and beneath the skin\".","Stated: \"the frog's heart stops beating\".","Stated: \"it does not breathe\"."]),
 Q("What is the main purpose of paragraph 2?","medium",["To compare the wood frog with animals that migrate in winter","To describe how the frog freezes and how glucose protects its cells","To explain how the frog finds temporary ponds for breeding in spring","To argue that large amounts of glucose damage the frog's cells"],1,"Paragraph 2 explains where ice forms, what stops, and how glucose keeps cells from shrinking to a fatal degree.",["The comparison with migrating animals is made in paragraph 1.","Breeding in ponds is mentioned briefly in paragraph 3, and finding ponds is not explained.","The passage says glucose protects the cells, not that it damages them."])]))
P.append(("P02","Earth Science","""In lakes fed by melting glaciers, the mud on the bottom often builds up in thin, paired layers. Each spring and summer, meltwater streams carry sand and silt into the lake, and these relatively coarse particles settle quickly, forming a light-colored band. In winter, when the streams freeze and the lake's surface is covered by ice, the water becomes still, and the finest clay particles, which had remained suspended, slowly sink to form a thinner, darker band. One light band and one dark band together make up a varve, a deposit representing a single year.

Because each varve records one year, counting varves works much like counting tree rings. In the late nineteenth century, the Swedish geologist Gerard De Geer began measuring varves exposed in Swedish clay deposits. He noticed that the thickness of successive varves rose and fell in recognizable sequences, reflecting warm years with heavy melting and cold years with little. By matching these sequences from one site to the next, he linked overlapping records into a single chronology. Extended by later researchers, it covers roughly the last 13,000 years, the period during which the great ice sheet retreated across Sweden.

The method requires care. A sudden flood or an underwater landslide can deposit a layer that resembles an annual band, and a year with very little sediment may leave a layer too thin to see. Researchers therefore check varve counts against independent methods, such as radiocarbon dating of plant remains preserved in the layers.""",[
 Q("The word \"suspended\" in the passage is closest in meaning to","medium",["temporarily stopped","hanging from a fixed support","floating in the water","removed from a post as a punishment"],2,"The clay particles \"had remained suspended\" in the water and then \"slowly sink\" once the water is still.",["Nothing is paused or interrupted; the particles are in the water.","The particles are in the lake water, not attached to anything.","No person or position is involved."]),
 Q("According to paragraph 1, why does a dark band form in winter?","medium",["Streams carry more sand and silt into the lake in cold weather.","Ice cover leaves the water still, so fine clay can settle.","Plant remains darken the sediment as they decay.","Coarse particles sink more slowly in cold water."],1,"\"when the streams freeze and the lake's surface is covered by ice, the water becomes still, and the finest clay particles ... slowly sink.\"",["Streams carry sand and silt in spring and summer; in winter they freeze.","Plant remains are mentioned only in paragraph 3, as material for radiocarbon dating.","Coarse particles settle quickly in summer; the winter band is fine clay."]),
 Q("Why does the author mention \"counting tree rings\"?","hard",["To compare varve counting with a familiar dating method","To identify the independent method used to check varve counts","To show that tree rings are more accurate than varves","To explain how De Geer first became interested in geology"],0,"Each varve, like a tree ring, records one year, so the comparison explains how varves are counted.",["The independent check named in paragraph 3 is radiocarbon dating, not tree rings.","The accuracy of the two methods is not compared.","De Geer's interests are not discussed."]),
 Q("It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that an unusually thick varve most likely indicates","hard",["a cold year with little melting","a year when no clay settled to the bottom","a summer in which the lake stayed frozen","a warm year with heavy melting"],3,"Varve thickness rose and fell \"reflecting warm years with heavy melting and cold years with little\"; heavy melting brings more sediment and a thicker layer.",["Cold years with little melting are linked to less sediment, so thinner varves.","A varve by definition includes a dark clay band; a year without one is not described.","Summer is when streams carry sediment in; a lake frozen all summer is never described and would not thicken a varve."]),
 Q("Which of the following best describes the purpose of paragraph 3?","medium",["To describe how the ice sheet retreated across Sweden","To note possible errors in varve counts and how they are checked","To argue that radiocarbon dating should replace varve counting","To explain how floods produced the clay deposits De Geer studied"],1,"It names floods, landslides, and very thin years as sources of miscounting, and radiocarbon dating as the check.",["The retreat of the ice sheet is mentioned in paragraph 2.","Radiocarbon dating is used to check varve counts, not to replace them.","Floods are a source of misleading layers, not the origin of De Geer's deposits."])]))
P.append(("P03","History of Technology","""Until the second half of the nineteenth century, most towns kept their own time. Clocks were set by the sun, so that noon was the moment the sun stood highest over that particular place. Because the Earth turns through one degree of longitude about every four minutes, a town some distance east of its neighbor would be several minutes ahead of it. For people who rarely traveled far, these differences hardly mattered.

The railways changed that. A train passing through many towns could not follow all of their clocks at once, and timetables printed in local time were confusing and, where trains shared a single track, dangerous. Railway companies responded by adopting standards of their own. In Britain, most lines had adopted London time by the late 1840s. In the United States, where distances were far greater, individual railroads used dozens of different standards, and a large station might display several clocks, each showing the time kept by a different company.

On November 18, 1883, the major railroads of the United States and Canada replaced these with a small number of time zones, each an hour apart, so that all clocks within a zone agreed. Many cities adopted railroad time quickly, although some resisted what they saw as interference with the natural time set by the sun. Standard time was the railroads' own arrangement, however; it did not become federal law in the United States until 1918.""",[
 Q("The word \"arrangement\" in the passage is closest in meaning to","medium",["a piece of music adapted for other instruments","a plan agreed on by the parties involved","a decorative grouping of cut flowers","the positioning of furniture in a room"],1,"The railroads agreed on standard time among themselves; it was not yet law.",["Music is not discussed.","Flowers are not discussed.","Furniture is not discussed; the sense here is an agreed system."]),
 Q("It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that the clocks of a town east of its neighbor were ahead because","hard",["its clocks were set by a railway company's standard","it lay closer to a major city such as London","the sun reached its highest point there earlier","its residents traveled farther than their neighbors"],2,"Noon was set when \"the sun stood highest over that particular place\", and as the Earth turns, places farther east reach that moment first.",["Paragraph 1 describes the period before railway standards, when clocks were set by the sun.","Distance from London is not mentioned in paragraph 1.","The passage says travel was rare and the differences \"hardly mattered\"; travel does not cause them."]),
 Q("Why does the author mention that a large station \"might display several clocks\"?","hard",["To show that American stations were larger than British ones","To suggest that local sun time was more accurate than railroad time","To describe how some cities opposed railroad time","To illustrate how many competing time standards were in use"],3,"Each clock showed \"the time kept by a different company\", illustrating the \"dozens of different standards\".",["The sizes of stations in the two countries are not compared.","The accuracy of sun time is not compared with railroad time.","Opposition by cities is described in paragraph 3, after the 1883 change."]),
 Q("According to the passage, all of the following are true of the change made on November 18, 1883, EXCEPT:","hard",["It was organized by the railroads rather than by law.","It was accepted at once by every city it affected.","It included railroads in Canada as well as the United States.","It created zones in which clocks were an hour apart."],1,"\"Many cities adopted railroad time quickly, although some resisted.\"",["Stated: \"Standard time was the railroads' own arrangement\" and became law only in 1918.","Stated: \"the major railroads of the United States and Canada\".","Stated: \"time zones, each an hour apart\"."]),
 Q("Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?","medium",["The needs of the railways led to standard time zones replacing local sun time.","Britain adopted standard time first because its railways were older.","Federal law in 1918 created the time zones used by American railroads.","Most cities defended local sun time as more accurate than railroad time."],0,"Paragraph 1 describes local sun time, paragraph 2 the problems trains created, and paragraph 3 the zones that replaced it.",["Britain's earlier adoption is a detail, and no reason based on the age of its railways is given.","The railroads created the zones in 1883; the 1918 law came later.","Only \"some\" cities resisted, and accuracy is not the reason given."])]))
P.append(("P04","Psychology","""In a well-known experiment published in 1999, the psychologists Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris asked participants to watch a short video of two teams passing basketballs and to count the passes made by the players wearing white. Partway through, a person in a gorilla costume walked into the middle of the scene, paused, and walked off. Roughly half of the viewers, absorbed in counting, did not notice the gorilla at all, and many were astonished when the video was replayed.

The phenomenon, which the researchers Arien Mack and Irvin Rock had named inattentional blindness, shows that seeing is not simply a matter of having an object in front of one's eyes. Attention acts as a filter. When it is fully occupied by a demanding task, objects outside that task can pass through the field of view without being consciously registered, even when they are large, unusual, and in plain sight. Importantly, the effect depends on what the observer is attending to. In related studies, unexpected objects that resembled the items being tracked were noticed more often than objects that differed from them.

The finding has practical implications. A driver talking on a phone, for instance, may look directly at a hazard and still fail to respond to it. At the same time, people tend to be confident that they would notice anything important that happened in front of them, a belief the experiment suggests is often mistaken.""",[
 Q("The word \"registered\" in the passage is closest in meaning to","medium",["officially recorded on a list","sent by guaranteed mail","shown on a measuring instrument","perceived"],3,"Objects can pass through the field of view without being \"consciously registered\", that is, without being noticed.",["No official list is involved; the sense concerns awareness.","Mail is not discussed.","No instrument is involved; the sentence concerns conscious awareness."]),
 Q("According to paragraph 2, which unexpected objects were noticed more often?","medium",["objects that were larger than the items being tracked","objects that appeared near the edge of the scene","objects that resembled the items being tracked","objects that moved more quickly than the players"],2,"\"unexpected objects that resembled the items being tracked were noticed more often than objects that differed from them.\"",["The passage says objects can be missed \"even when they are large\".","Position in the scene is not discussed.","Speed of movement is not discussed."]),
 Q("Why does the author mention a driver talking on a phone?","medium",["To argue that phone use causes most accidents","To give an example of the effect in everyday life","To show that the effect disappears when a task is familiar","To contrast confident people with those who doubt themselves"],1,"It follows \"The finding has practical implications\" and illustrates the effect outside the laboratory.",["No claim is made about the share of accidents caused by phones.","Familiar tasks are not discussed.","The confidence point is a separate observation about people in general."]),
 Q("Which of the following can be inferred from paragraph 2 about the viewers who missed the gorilla?","hard",["They had poorer eyesight than viewers who noticed it.","They were not paying attention to the video at all.","They had seen the video before the experiment began.","The gorilla may have been in view yet unseen."],3,"Seeing \"is not simply a matter of having an object in front of one's eyes\"; objects can pass through the field of view without being registered.",["The passage says the effect is about attention, not the eyes.","They were attending closely to the video, \"absorbed in counting\" passes.","Nothing suggests prior viewing; many \"were astonished\" on replay."]),
 Q("According to paragraph 1, all of the following are true of the 1999 experiment EXCEPT:","medium",["Viewers counted the passes made by both teams.","The players in the video passed basketballs.","A person in a gorilla costume walked through the scene.","About half the viewers failed to notice the gorilla."],0,"Viewers counted only \"the passes made by the players wearing white\".",["Stated: \"two teams passing basketballs\".","Stated: \"a person in a gorilla costume walked into the middle of the scene\".","Stated: \"Roughly half of the viewers ... did not notice the gorilla\"."])]))
P.append(("P05","Economics","""In 1959, a very large natural gas field was discovered near Groningen in the northern Netherlands. Over the following years, gas exports brought substantial income into the country. Yet by the 1970s, observers noticed that Dutch manufacturing was struggling, and in 1977 the magazine The Economist gave the pattern a name: the Dutch disease. The term now describes a broader puzzle, in which a boom in one natural-resource sector appears to weaken other parts of an economy.

Economists usually explain the effect through prices. When foreign buyers pay for a country's resource exports, they must acquire its currency, and this added demand tends to push up the currency's value. A stronger currency makes the country's other exports, such as manufactured goods, more expensive abroad, while making imported goods cheaper at home. Domestic manufacturers are squeezed from both sides. At the same time, the booming sector may draw workers and investment away from other industries by offering higher wages.

The difficulty becomes clearest when the boom ends. Resources may run out or their world price may fall, and the industries that shrank during the boom cannot always be rebuilt quickly, because skills and businesses that were lost take years to replace. For this reason, some resource-rich governments have tried to limit the effect by saving part of their resource income abroad, often in large state funds, rather than spending all of it at home, which eases the pressure on the currency.""",[
 Q("The word \"squeezed\" in the passage is closest in meaning to","medium",["put under financial pressure","pressed firmly between the fingers","fitted into a very small space","obtained by force or threats"],0,"Manufacturers lose sales abroad and face cheaper imports at home, so they are under pressure \"from both sides\".",["The sense is economic, not physical pressing.","Nothing is being fitted into a space.","Nothing is being taken from the manufacturers by threats."]),
 Q("According to paragraph 2, how does a resource boom raise the value of a country's currency?","medium",["The government prints less money during a boom.","Foreign buyers need the currency to pay for the exports.","Imported goods become more expensive at home.","Manufacturers raise prices to match higher wages."],1,"Foreign buyers \"must acquire its currency, and this added demand tends to push up the currency's value.\"",["Printing money is not mentioned.","The passage says imports become cheaper, not more expensive.","Wages are discussed separately and are not linked to the currency's value."]),
 Q("It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that during a resource boom, a domestic car manufacturer would most likely face","hard",["lower wages for the workers it employs","a weaker currency that boosts its exports","higher domestic prices for natural gas","stronger competition from cheaper imports"],3,"A stronger currency makes \"imported goods cheaper at home\", so imported cars compete more easily.",["The booming sector offers higher wages, which pushes wages up, not down.","The boom strengthens the currency, which makes exports more expensive abroad.","Domestic gas prices are not discussed."]),
 Q("Why does the author mention that \"skills and businesses that were lost take years to replace\"?","hard",["To suggest that foreign workers should be hired during a boom","To argue that resource booms usually last many years","To explain why lost industries are slow to recover","To show why manufacturing wages rise during a boom"],2,"It gives the reason that \"the industries that shrank during the boom cannot always be rebuilt quickly.\"",["Hiring foreign workers is not discussed.","The paragraph is about what happens when booms end, not how long they last.","Wages are discussed in paragraph 2, and the phrase concerns the period after the boom."]),
 Q("What is the main purpose of paragraph 1?","medium",["To present the case that gave an economic pattern its name","To explain why the Groningen gas field was discovered so late","To argue that the Netherlands managed its gas income well","To describe how a strong currency harms manufacturers"],0,"It describes the Dutch gas discovery, the struggles of Dutch manufacturing, and the naming of \"the Dutch disease\".",["No suggestion is made that the discovery came late.","The paragraph notes that manufacturing was struggling, not that income was managed well.","The currency mechanism is explained in paragraph 2."])]))
P.append(("P06","Art History","""In true fresco, or buon fresco, the painter applies pigments mixed only with water to a layer of wet lime plaster. As the plaster dries, the lime reacts with carbon dioxide in the air and forms a crystalline layer of calcium carbonate that locks the pigment particles into the surface of the wall. Because the color becomes part of the plaster rather than resting on top of it, a well-made fresco can survive for centuries.

The technique imposes strict demands. Since the paint binds only while the plaster is damp, the artist could plaster only as much wall as could be painted in a single session, an area Italian painters called a giornata, or "day's work." The joins between these patches are often still visible to conservators, allowing them to estimate how many days a mural took. Mistakes were difficult to correct; the usual remedy was to cut away the plaster and begin that section again. In addition, some pigments, including certain blues, did not work well in wet lime and had to be added later, on the dry surface, in a method known as secco.

Painting on dry plaster gave artists more time and a wider range of colors, but the paint never bonded with the wall in the same way. Leonardo da Vinci painted The Last Supper in Milan on a dry wall rather than in true fresco, and the painting began to deteriorate within a few decades. It has required repeated restoration ever since.""",[
 Q("The word \"imposes\" in the passage is closest in meaning to","hard",["takes unfair advantage of","places as a requirement","arranges pages for printing","enters without being invited"],1,"The technique \"imposes strict demands\": it places requirements on the painter, which the paragraph then lists.",["Nobody's goodwill is being exploited; the sentence is about requirements.","Printing is not discussed.","Nothing enters anywhere; the technique places demands on the artist."]),
 Q("According to paragraph 2, why could an artist plaster only a limited area at a time?","medium",["Lime plaster was too costly to use in large amounts.","Wet plaster was too heavy to stay on a high wall.","The paint would bind only while the plaster was damp.","Conservators needed to see the joins between patches."],2,"\"Since the paint binds only while the plaster is damp, the artist could plaster only as much wall as could be painted in a single session.\"",["The cost of plaster is not mentioned.","The weight of plaster is not mentioned.","The visible joins are a result of the method, discovered later, not its cause."]),
 Q("Why does the author mention that the joins between patches \"are often still visible to conservators\"?","hard",["To argue that most frescoes were poorly made","To explain why mistakes were difficult to correct","To suggest that secco paint flaked away at the joins","To show how a mural's painting time can be estimated"],3,"The visible joins allow conservators \"to estimate how many days a mural took.\"",["The passage says well-made frescoes survive for centuries; it does not call them poorly made.","The difficulty of corrections is a separate point in the next sentence.","Secco paint is discussed later and is not linked to the joins."]),
 Q("It can be inferred from the passage that the blues added \"on the dry surface\" would be","hard",["less firmly bonded to the wall than fresco colors","more permanent than colors bound into wet plaster","visible only to conservators under close examination","applied before the wet plaster was laid on the wall"],0,"Paragraph 3: paint on dry plaster \"never bonded with the wall in the same way.\"",["The passage implies the opposite: colors in true fresco last longest.","Only the joins are said to be visible to conservators.","Secco colors were \"added later, on the dry surface\", after the plaster was laid and dried."]),
 Q("How does paragraph 3 relate to paragraph 2?","hard",["It rejects the claim that true fresco can last for centuries.","It gives a second example of a mural that took many days to paint.","It weighs the dry method's gains and costs, using a famous case.","It explains how conservators repaired the joins between patches."],2,"Paragraph 2 ends by introducing secco; paragraph 3 gives its advantages (time, colors) and its weakness (weak bond), with The Last Supper as the example.",["The claim about centuries is made in paragraph 1 and is not rejected.","No mural is described as taking many days in paragraph 3.","Repairs to the joins are not discussed; The Last Supper's restoration concerns deterioration."])]))
out=[]
for pid,subj,text,qs in P:
    for i,q in enumerate(qs,1):
        dist=[c for c in q["choices"] if c!=q["correct_answer"]]
        out.append({"id":f"PD2-{pid}-{i}","type":"multiple_choice","passage":text,"passageGroupId":f"pd2-{pid}","prompt":f"[Academic — {subj}] {q['prompt']}","choices":q["choices"],"correct_answer":q["correct_answer"],"difficulty":q["difficulty"],"readingTask":"academic_passage","explanation":q["explanation"],"distractor_rationales":[{"choice":c,"reason":r} for c,r in zip(dist,q["rats"])],"blanks":None,"graphic":None,"correct_answers":None,"acceptable_answers":None})
json.dump(out,open('items.json','w'),indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)
for pid,subj,text,qs in P: print(pid,subj,len(text.split()),"paras",len([p for p in text.split("\n\n") if p.strip()]))
lo=sh=0;slots=[0,0,0,0];dc={}
for x in out:
    L=[len(c) for c in x["choices"]];mx=max(L);mn=min(L)
    if len(x["correct_answer"])==mx and L.count(mx)==1: lo+=1;print("  longest",x["id"])
    if len(x["correct_answer"])==mn and L.count(mn)==1: sh+=1;print("  shortest",x["id"])
    slots[x["choices"].index(x["correct_answer"])]+=1;dc[x["difficulty"]]=dc.get(x["difficulty"],0)+1
print(len(out),"longest",lo,"shortest",sh,"slots",slots,dc)
